<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bukovina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bukovina is a historical region on the north-eastern slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a>, divided today between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Part of the Principality of Moldavia, it was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy in 1775 and became a separate crown land in 1849, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernivtsi">Czernowitz</a>. It joined Romania in 1918 and was split in 1940, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> annexed the north.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bukovina.jpg" alt="Bukovina" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adilena · CC BY-SA 3.0 ro · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Voronet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
